---
import AppDocLayout from '../../layouts/AppDocLayout.astro';
---

<AppDocLayout
  title="Tune Easy - Support"
  description="Get help with Tune Easy - Simple Guitar Tuner iOS app."
  ogUrl="https://idlefusion.com/tune-easy/support"
>
  <main>
    <section class="section-padding">
      <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-8">Support</h1>

        <div class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed mb-12">
          <p class="text-lg mb-4">Need help with Tune Easy?</p>
          <p>Check the frequently asked questions below, or send us an email at <a href="mailto:support@idlefusion.com?subject=Tune%20Easy%20Support%20Request" class="text-[var(--accent-primary)] hover:underline">support@idlefusion.com</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="faq-item">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Why isn't the tuner detecting my guitar?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">Tune Easy needs the microphone to hear your strings. If you chose not to allow it, the home screen says "Microphone is off" and has an Open Settings button; you can also turn it on in Settings → Privacy & Security → Microphone. Then play a little closer to your iPhone, in a quieter room.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">The tuner shows the wrong note. What's happening?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">Tune Easy matches what it hears to the nearest string of the tuning you picked. If a string is more than three semitones off, or you're in a different tuning, it may read as another string. Check the tuning above the headline, bring the string roughly into range by ear (a reference note helps), then fine-tune with the dial.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">What is "cents" and why does it matter?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">A cent is 1/100th of a semitone — a tiny unit of pitch. When the display shows +5 or -10 cents, that's how far you are from perfect pitch. Most people can't hear differences smaller than about 5 cents, so anywhere in that range sounds "in tune."</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Does Tune Easy support alternate tunings?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">Yes. Tap the tuning above the headline to pick one of eleven: Standard, half step down, full step down, Drop D, Drop C, Drop B, Open G, Open D, Open E, Open C and DADGAD. Tune Easy listens for the strings of the tuning you pick. For any other instrument, open the menu and choose Chromatic, which names any note.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">What are reference tones?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">Tap any string button at the bottom of the screen to hear that string's note in the current tuning, played as a short plucked tone. While it plays, the tuner pauses so it doesn't hear your iPhone instead of your guitar.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Does this work with acoustic and electric guitars?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">Yes. Tune Easy works with any guitar that produces audible sound. For electric guitars, you can either play unplugged (the strings are usually loud enough) or hold your device near your amp's speaker.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Can I tune to A = 432 Hz or match a piano?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">Yes. Open the menu, then Settings, and set the reference pitch anywhere from A4 = 425 to 455 Hz. Most music uses 440 Hz, the default.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Does Tune Easy record my audio?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">No. Audio from your microphone is processed in real time for pitch detection only. Nothing is recorded, stored, or sent anywhere. The audio data only exists in temporary memory while the app is analyzing pitch.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-2">Why is the dial jumping around?</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] leading-relaxed">This usually happens when there's background noise or when the string is still vibrating with unwanted overtones. Try plucking the string cleanly and letting it ring in a quieter space.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</AppDocLayout>
